kubectl get po -w (watch)
kubectl describe pod (view)

In Kubernetes, there are two types of accounts
  User Account   ( used to login into cluster )
  Service Account  ( to communicate service to service or resource to resource )
    User account: User accounts are used to log into a Kubernetes cluster and manipulate resources therein. Each user account is 
associated with a unique set of credentials, which are used to authenticate the service’s requests.
    Service Account: Kubernetes Service Accounts are specialised accounts used by applications and services 
running on Kubernetes to interact with the Kubernetes API.

There are four components to RBAC in Kubernetes:
  1) roles   - A role defines a set of permissions for a user or group. For example, you might create a role called “admin” that gives the user or group full access to all resources in the cluster.
  2) Cluster roles  ( have permissons for all name spaces in cluster)
  3) role bindings    ( have permiison on single namespce)
  4) ClusterRolesBinding

